{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/src/storage.rs",
  "item": "mod[tests]/impl[FaultyTemporary/Write]/fn[flush]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Write requires flush, but this unbuffered fault-injection fixture\n tests write_all, replacement, and cleanup. write_and_replace does\n not request flushing or durability; real temporary files are\n unbuffered too. A flush call would change the tested operation\n sequence, so the unused callback is an explicit guard."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/storage.rs` `mod[tests]/impl[FaultyTemporary/Write]/fn[flush]` is a fenced commentary citation on an explicit function with a body. That is a permitted form.\n\nThe commentary is substantive and matches the current source:\n- `fn[write_and_replace]` calls only `temporary.write_all(bytes)`, `temporary.replace(target)` and `temporary.cleanup()`. It never calls `flush`, and it never asks for durability or syncing.\n- The fixture records operations as \"write\" and \"replace\" through `FaultyTemporary`'s `write` and `TemporaryRecord::replace`. The test `writing_and_replacing_report_each_io_failure_and_cleanup_failure` asserts the exact operation sequences. A `flush` call would therefore change the sequence being tested.\n- `flush` only exists because the `Write` trait requires it. It is deliberately a panic guard, so the zero execution is expected, and the guard fails the test if `write_and_replace` ever starts flushing.\n\nThe explanation therefore gives a real reason the unexecuted function is acceptable. It is not just a claim that covering it is difficult. I found no gap.",
